WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.620
LARRY P. ARNN: It's
in your choosing

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:04.080
that your character
is formed, which

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.192
gives you the right kind
of direction in your life.

00:00:08.192 --> 00:00:13.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:17.910
Choice and deliberation.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:22.450
Give me an example of an
unwilling act that we do?

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:24.210
SPEAKER 2: Well, like
when I was younger,

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.500
and you had to
clean up-- your mom

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.000
made you clean up
your room, and you

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.470
didn't want to
clean up your room,

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.840
but you did it because
you were forced to.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.900
LARRY P. ARNN: That is, by
the way, a perfect example.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.080
Because the hard
choices typically

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.380
involve some element of duress.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:46.230
So when you're a child, your
parents do that for you.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.690
In the end, there really isn't
an option when you're not

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.680
of age, because you're a long
way from being mature enough

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:57.210
to take off and leave
them and might not

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.220
be able to make it
stick if you did.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.189
What is the process by
which choices are produced?

00:01:03.189 --> 00:01:05.064
SPEAKER 3: Is that what
we call deliberation?

00:01:05.064 --> 00:01:05.920
LARRY P. ARNN: It is.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:06.462
Explain that.

00:01:06.462 --> 00:01:08.530
SPEAKER 3: So
deliberation, instead

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:11.890
of debating about what the
ends are of our actions,

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:15.280
deliberation is about selecting
the best means by which we

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.416
attain those ends.

00:01:17.416 --> 00:01:20.650
LARRY P. ARNN: So deliberation
is not about ends.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:22.861
Where do ends come from?

00:01:22.861 --> 00:01:24.770
SPEAKER 4: Our nature.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:27.260
LARRY P. ARNN: Our nature.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.600
Our nature cultivated?

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.490
SPEAKER 5: It says wish.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.460
So ends are wished for.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.510
ANd depending on
your nature, you're

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:39.560
going to wish for
better or worse ends

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:40.890
because of what they desire.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:44.185
So a good nature will desire
better ends than a bad nature.

00:01:44.185 --> 00:01:49.850
LARRY P. ARNN: It's this demand
of leading a life of character,

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.920
an ethical life.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.410
It's in your choosing that
your character is formed,

00:01:57.410 --> 00:02:02.240
which gives you the right kind
of direction in your life.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.420
We're about to see
when we separate

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:08.699
each act into its
component parts,

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:10.910
it's always made
up of two things--

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:16.190
what you want and an estimation
of the circumstances.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:20.240
And what you want is
achieved, the right thing,

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:26.120
by practice and thought about
what the right way to live is,

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.160
and what the right
kind of person is,

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.080
and about what the
ultimate ends are.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.180
And remember, you're making
these deliberations--

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.790
if they're the kind that are
meaningful in the shaping

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:42.560
of your character, you're
making them under pressure.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.210
It means there are stakes.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:47.000
It means that you
might get hurt or fail

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.460
to get some large benefit.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.310
It means that you're
likely to be tired.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:57.770
You have to decide, right
now, and you can never

00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:01.280
be sure because you can
never be sure of the future.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.430
You can never be sure of the
consequences what you do.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:07.610
You have to make a guess.

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:10.170
And you get better
at that by trying it.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.470
But the guessing part itself--

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.347
because what matters
the most-- what

00:03:14.347 --> 00:03:16.430
is the thing, when you
make a choice, that matters

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:19.730
the most about whether it
tends to build virtue in you

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:22.071
or not-- what is that thing?

00:03:22.071 --> 00:03:23.788
SPEAKER 6: Intention.

00:03:23.788 --> 00:03:25.650
LARRY P. ARNN: Intention.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.328
What do you want?

00:03:27.328 --> 00:03:29.120
When you get older,
you'll figure this out.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.100
The what you want part,
when you make a choice,

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:38.570
will be more important to
how you remember the choice,

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:42.620
and how you regard yourself,
and what specifically you chose

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:46.010
to do, and what the result was.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.046
You ever crash a car?

00:03:48.046 --> 00:03:50.246
SPEAKER 3: Yep.

00:03:50.246 --> 00:03:54.180
LARRY P. ARNN: Any of the
girls ever crashed a car?

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:54.870
Yeah, see.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.210
Don't we resent that, guys?

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.120
It doesn't seem fair, but it
does seem true, doesn't it?

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.770
I crashed them all the
time when I was a kid.

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:06.270
Why?

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:09.630
Because I didn't-- I never
crashed one bad, but--

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.520
and my dad was not a rich man.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.360
I'd break his cars.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:14.760
And why?

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:19.649
I was young and stupid,
inexperienced, you see.

00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:23.820
So just remember, even in this
formative time in your life,

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:31.140
what you see is the thing
that has to be got right.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:34.110
And if you get used to that,
you'll get better and better

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:35.970
at the job of seeking it.

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:37.583
That's what changes
your character.

00:04:37.583 --> 00:04:40.250
That's what shapes it, and forms
it, and makes you what you are.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:46.720
I'm Larry Arnn, president
of Hillsdale College.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.090
You will find, if you look
at our online courses,

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:51.700
that they cover the whole
range of human learning,

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:55.210
all the high and fundamental
things everyone needs to know.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:57.490
They are free because
we have always

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:00.460
wished to provide sound
learning to anyone

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:02.790
who wishes to have it.

